#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1980ddeb-37bf-5234-8888-f8292e2c9405" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a Second-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1602d4cf-6f96-55a1-9aa5-af0e750fffaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-2(n+1)(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358219} {:answer/id #uuid "563dbd2e-ece6-50cd-9efb-c52e41d8e252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=-\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358220} {:answer/id #uuid "1b33b297-5e39-5b84-9c11-20bad5561b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358221} {:answer/id #uuid "151db3f9-258e-5d5b-b887-b6ace8346d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=2n(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358222} {:answer/id #uuid "4799e5ad-19e2-55fc-8842-686df215e6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=2(n+1)(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4799e5ad-19e2-55fc-8842-686df215e6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n)=2(n+1)(n+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358223} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccc29b0b-638a-5b35-a5d4-a57711a38aac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5bd1188-011f-5de8-8224-47cb9fd29c4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-101389" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $18{y}^{″}-9y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n18{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}-9{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n18{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}-9{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n18{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}-9{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[18(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-9{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n18(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-9{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{2(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=2(n+1)(n+2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ab3f10-aca4-5094-98d2-0de9c11fd711" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050a1396-0a09-55cb-96b9-e4be221a86fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{5{a}_{n}}{2(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358226} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8f18fc-83b2-5020-93c4-f82f0a57f697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=10{a}_{n}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358227} {:answer/id #uuid "3303e07a-ba8e-5b03-b423-d30ca1b4908a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2{a}_{n-1}}{5(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358228} {:answer/id #uuid "badd6234-c1a5-5521-8a70-271e206dd05c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{5(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358229} {:answer/id #uuid "234f91be-a0ab-557d-9ca2-cde2074029b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{5n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3303e07a-ba8e-5b03-b423-d30ca1b4908a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2{a}_{n-1}}{5(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358228} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e663ad7-1dab-574f-a964-40d082646d40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b4a15be5-56fd-581a-a9cd-9c8be7b9791e" :question/math-academy-id "q-101403" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $5{y}^{′}-2xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-2x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }5(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }5(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n5{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n5{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[5(n+1){a}_{n+1}-2{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,5(n+1){a}_{n+1}=2{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2{a}_{n-1}}{5(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ab3f10-aca4-5094-98d2-0de9c11fd711" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ab77680-db2f-5819-961b-2d868d057602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358233} {:answer/id #uuid "3a067f6b-3a66-577b-987e-a04ed4cd85ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358234} {:answer/id #uuid "0abd4e93-6c73-5a00-b2f1-3b79c4965074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-9{a}_{n-1}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358235} {:answer/id #uuid "2155f10e-a88d-5885-8ceb-f8baa45f3603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358236} {:answer/id #uuid "ad71331c-fca7-592e-9d2c-7ad49bd3901c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2155f10e-a88d-5885-8ceb-f8baa45f3603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9(n+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358236} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdae2540-94a1-5d66-8c47-0d41668d85d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9acbfa33-35c8-5eb4-af30-be51fe0a0220" :question/math-academy-id "q-102315" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $xy+9{y}^{′}=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+9{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}+{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }9(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }9(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }9(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}+9{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n9{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[{a}_{n-1}+9(n+1){a}_{n+1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,9(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{9(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ab3f10-aca4-5094-98d2-0de9c11fd711" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a First-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b1152e6-383e-5d08-abe6-97bf69210f0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{-2{a}_{n-1}}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358240} {:answer/id #uuid "48be2863-d777-5985-a3e8-e70c96674496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=-2{a}_{n}(n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358241} {:answer/id #uuid "50cb3ecf-79d9-5218-8159-ad90d4b8e074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{-2{a}_{n}}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358242} {:answer/id #uuid "635e5672-d098-5348-9c7d-4c8d0e0d0cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2{a}_{n-1}}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358243} {:answer/id #uuid "d9845efa-ee87-53ff-bd51-26f4dcda4dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{-2{a}_{n-1}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b1152e6-383e-5d08-abe6-97bf69210f0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{-2{a}_{n-1}}{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358240} :answer-field/id #uuid "5259d813-0292-5c81-95f1-2971bcdd7542" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "01e4a118-39ab-5f83-a84a-4e3bea205b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-365776" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{′}+2xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3…$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+2x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[(n+1){a}_{n+1}+2{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-2{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{-2{a}_{n-1}}{n+1},\\,n=1,2,3….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1980ddeb-37bf-5234-8888-f8292e2c9405" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Recurrence Relation For a Second-Order Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8feb4351-9883-530e-a4cf-8bb3fc1e86f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n) = (n + 1)(2n + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146666} {:answer/id #uuid "f520cc49-3944-502a-8c0f-b6b0bf212f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n) = (n + 1)(n + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146667} {:answer/id #uuid "ff982693-169a-5664-96a1-a992f99410a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n) = (n + 1)(n + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146668} {:answer/id #uuid "3ddf1315-ec80-5ebc-b560-6294dd5751ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n) = n(n + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146669} {:answer/id #uuid "7290a722-3f39-5921-ada3-a826c377c7b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n) = n(n + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff982693-169a-5664-96a1-a992f99410a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(n) = (n + 1)(n + 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146668} :answer-field/id #uuid "50ddaf93-c6bf-529b-a371-999ac59f25aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb0eb766-31b8-5c5b-be92-35f9333b800c" :question/math-academy-id "q-64515" :question/problem "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=(n+1)(n+2).$"}]}
